---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Corvelle - Privacy Policy"
  description="Privacy policy for Corvelle, the iPhone app for an AI assistant you host on your own server."
>
  <h1>Corvelle Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="meta">Effective 29 October 2026</p>

  <p>
    Corvelle is an iPhone app for chatting with an AI assistant that runs on a
    server you control (or that someone you trust runs for you).
    <strong>The developer collects no data about you.</strong> There are no
    accounts with us, no analytics, no tracking, no ads, and no third-party
    SDKs. What you send in the app goes to your own server, not to us.
  </p>

  <h2>Data we collect</h2>
  <p>
    None. Corvelle has no analytics, crash reporting, or tracking of any kind,
    and it never sends your messages, reminders, calendar, or pictures to the
    developer. The only time we receive anything from you is when you email us
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>What goes to your server</h2>
  <p>
    Corvelle talks to one server: the broker you pair it with. The broker
    passes your requests on to your assistant. Whoever runs that server
    decides how long it keeps what it receives.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>When you pair:</strong> your device's name, the app version, a
      device public key that identifies this iPhone to your server, and the
      pairing code. The private key stays in the iPhone's Secure Enclave.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When you chat:</strong> your messages, your local time and time
      zone (so "tomorrow at 9" means the right hour), and, if you have allowed
      Reminders access, a short list of your incomplete reminders (list names,
      titles, and due dates) so the assistant can answer questions about them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pictures you attach:</strong> only the photos, scans, or image
      files you pick for a message, with location and other metadata removed.
      They go to your server for that one reply and are not stored there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When you confirm or decline an action:</strong> whether it
      succeeded, and the identifier of the reminder it created.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The AI model</h2>
  <p>
    Your assistant uses a large language model to answer you. Depending on how
    your server is set up, that model may run on the server itself or at a
    third-party AI provider chosen by whoever runs the server. In that case
    your messages, attached pictures, and the reminder and calendar details
    you share are sent to that provider, under its own privacy policy, and may
    be kept in the assistant's history and memory. Corvelle shows this notice
    before your first chat; you can read it again in Settings &rsaquo; Privacy
    &rsaquo; AI notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Reminders and Calendar</h2>
  <p>
    With your permission, Corvelle reads your <em>incomplete</em> reminders to
    show them in the app and to give the assistant the short list described
    above. It creates a reminder only after you tap to confirm it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Calendar is off until you turn it on in Settings. Then the app reads your
    upcoming events to show them on Home. It never creates, edits, or deletes
    an event: "Add to Calendar" opens Apple's own editor, and nothing is saved
    unless you tap Add there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Calendar for the assistant</strong> is a separate setting, also off
    by default. When you turn it on and pick calendars, the app sends your
    server the events of those calendars in the next 48 hours: title, start
    and end time, whether it is all-day, and location, never notes or
    attendees. The server keeps only the latest copy and deletes it within 48
    hours, or at once when you turn the setting off. What the assistant has
    already read stays in its history.
  </p>

  <h2>Camera, photos, and files</h2>
  <p>
    The camera is used only while you take a photo or scan a document for a
    message. Photos and files are chosen in Apple's own pickers, so the app
    sees only what you pick and never browses your photo library.
  </p>

  <h2>Dictation</h2>
  <p>
    Dictation turns your speech into text on the iPhone itself. The audio is
    never recorded, stored, or sent anywhere. Only the text you then choose to
    send leaves the phone, like any typed message.
  </p>

  <h2>Maps, weather, and location</h2>
  <p>
    The assistant can show a place on a map or the weather for a place. Your
    iPhone asks Apple Maps or Apple Weather to draw it, so Apple receives the
    place the card is about (not your location). Apple handles these requests
    under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple's privacy
    policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Corvelle uses your location only when you tap the location button on a
    "Weather here" card, and only once. Your location, rounded to about 1 km,
    goes to Apple Weather for that one forecast. It is never stored, never
    tracked in the background, and never sent to your server, your assistant,
    or us.
  </p>

  <h2>Notifications</h2>
  <p>
    If you allow notifications, your server can tell you that a reply, a task
    result, or an approval is waiting. Notifications go through Apple's push
    service and never contain your messages. Where the App Store version uses
    the developer's notification relay, that relay receives only your device's
    push token, the content-free notice, and proof of your subscription, and
    has no access to your chats or your server.
  </p>

  <h2>Data on your iPhone</h2>
  <p>
    Your device key and access tokens are kept in the iPhone's Keychain, on
    this device only. A message that failed to send, or one you queued, is kept
    in the app's private storage so you can send it later, and is deleted once
    it is sent. Signing out deletes the tokens and device key; deleting the app
    removes everything else.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    Sign out in Settings to unpair this iPhone and delete its credentials.
    Anything your server or assistant has kept is under the control of whoever
    runs that server; ask them, or delete it there yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes in an important way, we will publish the new
    version at this address with a new date.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or concerns? Email
    <a href="mailto:corvelle@akitaka.me">corvelle@akitaka.me</a>, or open an issue at
    <a href="https://github.com/tarakanof/Corvelle-Site/issues">github.com/tarakanof/Corvelle-Site/issues</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
